WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.205 --> 00:00:02.145
Typhoid, pretty sure that's when you get

00:00:02.145 --> 00:00:03.917
like a knuckle in your knee.

00:00:03.917 --> 00:00:05.283
(up-beat music)

00:00:05.283 --> 00:00:08.033
(marker squeaks)

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:10.787
I have not taken the SAT before.

00:00:10.787 --> 00:00:12.109
I took the ACT.

00:00:12.109 --> 00:00:14.573
I've done prep for both
the SAT and the ACT.

00:00:14.573 --> 00:00:17.702
I have never taken the
SAT, but in high school

00:00:17.702 --> 00:00:19.814
my junior year I did take the ACT.

00:00:19.814 --> 00:00:23.407
I took the SAT once, got
a eleven hundred on it.

00:00:23.407 --> 00:00:26.043
- [Interviewer] Do you know
when the first SAT was given?

00:00:26.043 --> 00:00:26.876
1945?

00:00:28.174 --> 00:00:29.007
(buzz)

00:00:29.007 --> 00:00:29.923
I don't know.

00:00:29.923 --> 00:00:30.756
1812
(buzz)

00:00:30.756 --> 00:00:32.368
1324
(buzz)

00:00:32.368 --> 00:00:33.680
400 B.C.
(buzz)

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.333
- [Interviewer] It was 1926.

00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:37.861
There's going to be questions
about like shoveling

00:00:37.861 --> 00:00:40.090
and like raising cattle or something.

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:42.307
A dealer bought a lot of three horses

00:00:42.307 --> 00:00:46.885
and some mules for $600,
he sold the lot for 820.

00:00:46.885 --> 00:00:50.275
If he gained $40 on each
horse, and $50 for each mule,

00:00:50.275 --> 00:00:51.621
how many mules were there?

00:00:51.621 --> 00:00:53.402
I feel like this would
be the type of question

00:00:53.402 --> 00:00:54.608
that people would make fun of.

00:00:54.608 --> 00:00:56.356
They'd just post memes about it.

00:00:56.356 --> 00:01:00.016
I could maybe see this in
a third grade math problem.

00:01:00.016 --> 00:01:02.607
To be quite honest, I don't
know like what a reasonable

00:01:02.607 --> 00:01:05.868
price for three horses and some mules are.

00:01:05.868 --> 00:01:07.183
Two mules is my final answer.

00:01:07.183 --> 00:01:08.016
(bell dings)

00:01:08.016 --> 00:01:09.227
Two of those were mules.

00:01:09.227 --> 00:01:10.094
(bell dings)

00:01:10.094 --> 00:01:11.859
I thought this was tricky.

00:01:11.859 --> 00:01:13.856
Premise, there are 12 months in a year,

00:01:13.856 --> 00:01:15.680
but Washington and Lincoln were born

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:17.365
in the same month, February.

00:01:17.365 --> 00:01:19.872
Conclusion, the two greatest
Americans of the next

00:01:19.872 --> 00:01:22.483
200 years will be born in February.

00:01:22.483 --> 00:01:24.405
Sure, Washington and
Lincoln, you know people knew

00:01:24.405 --> 00:01:25.427
their birthdays, but today,

00:01:25.427 --> 00:01:26.889
I didn't even know they
were born in February.

00:01:26.889 --> 00:01:28.309
There's a lot of great people in history.

00:01:28.309 --> 00:01:31.434
I know like Martin Luther
King Junior, Obama,

00:01:31.434 --> 00:01:33.085
Gandhi, oh but he's not American.

00:01:33.085 --> 00:01:34.334
Probably false.
(bell dings)

00:01:34.334 --> 00:01:35.416
Probably false.
(bell dings)

00:01:35.416 --> 00:01:36.758
Cause when I think of February today,

00:01:36.758 --> 00:01:38.074
I think of Martin Luther King.

00:01:38.074 --> 00:01:41.431
The statement that the moon
is made of green cheese is

00:01:41.431 --> 00:01:45.598
absurd, misleading,
improbable, unfair, or wicked.

00:01:46.463 --> 00:01:48.129
Does green cheese even exist?

00:01:48.129 --> 00:01:49.921
You can chose like blue
cheese, that's a real cheese.

00:01:49.921 --> 00:01:51.958
First of all, why would
this be on the SAT?

00:01:51.958 --> 00:01:54.613
What knowledge is this
gonna, like, enhance.

00:01:54.613 --> 00:01:55.542
Improbable.
(bell dings)

00:01:55.542 --> 00:01:56.983
It's a weird word choice,

00:01:56.983 --> 00:01:58.570
maybe it was used a lot back in the day.

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:01.746
Like, oh Hans, that's a wicked
cow you got on the field.

00:02:01.746 --> 00:02:02.579
(buzz)

00:02:02.579 --> 00:02:05.111
Improbable and absurd, I feel
like they go hand in hand.

00:02:05.111 --> 00:02:06.582
But because you're in high school,

00:02:06.582 --> 00:02:09.857
I hope that you know the moon
is not made of green cheese.

00:02:09.857 --> 00:02:13.591
If a package containing 20
cigarettes costs 15 cents,

00:02:13.591 --> 00:02:17.303
how many cigarettes can
be bought for 90 cents?

00:02:17.303 --> 00:02:18.837
This has to be back in 1920 something,

00:02:18.837 --> 00:02:23.020
cause cigarettes are definitely
costing way more 15 cents.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:24.598
This question would definitely not make it

00:02:24.598 --> 00:02:26.071
onto the real SATs.

00:02:26.071 --> 00:02:27.344
So 120 cigarettes is my answer.

00:02:27.344 --> 00:02:28.177
(bell dings)

00:02:28.177 --> 00:02:30.256
You would have to buy four cigarettes

00:02:30.256 --> 00:02:31.558
with 16 cigarettes left.

00:02:31.558 --> 00:02:32.524
(buzz)

00:02:32.524 --> 00:02:33.808
- [Interviewer] That is incorrect.

00:02:33.808 --> 00:02:35.391
Really? Oh, okay.

00:02:36.346 --> 00:02:41.029
If a man's salary is $20 a
week and he spends $14 a week,

00:02:41.029 --> 00:02:43.813
how long will it take him to save $300?

00:02:43.813 --> 00:02:46.011
I mean I guess it depends how old he is.

00:02:46.011 --> 00:02:47.344
Because if I was making $20,

00:02:47.344 --> 00:02:49.883
no wait no, I don't want
to be making $20 a week.

00:02:49.883 --> 00:02:51.163
What can you buy for $6?

00:02:51.163 --> 00:02:52.325
A pack of gum?

00:02:52.325 --> 00:02:53.893
That's not even a man, it has to be a kid.

00:02:53.893 --> 00:02:54.821
Probably doing chores.

00:02:54.821 --> 00:02:57.145
My final answer is 42
weeks and a couple days.

00:02:57.145 --> 00:02:58.278
(buzz)

00:02:58.278 --> 00:02:59.461
Fifty weeks.

00:02:59.461 --> 00:03:00.377
(bell dings)

00:03:00.377 --> 00:03:02.473
Don't SAT questions have
multiple choice anyway?

00:03:02.473 --> 00:03:03.494
I would have got 50 right.

00:03:03.494 --> 00:03:05.523
Two of the below four words are opposites

00:03:05.523 --> 00:03:08.523
or nearly opposites, pick those two.

00:03:09.513 --> 00:03:11.113
Opposites.

00:03:11.113 --> 00:03:12.613
Ecc-less-ee-uh-tal

00:03:13.619 --> 00:03:16.119
Intricate, obvious, and tepid.

00:03:17.853 --> 00:03:18.921
Cool.

00:03:18.921 --> 00:03:20.870
It's all about, you know,
taking out the words

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:22.481
you don't know, that's
what they wanted you to do.

00:03:22.481 --> 00:03:26.216
Uh-clec-li-as-cal, I think
that is a form of grain

00:03:26.216 --> 00:03:28.435
they used to farm in
the fields back in 1926.

00:03:28.435 --> 00:03:29.447
(buzz)

00:03:29.447 --> 00:03:30.280
Intricate and obvious.

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:31.211
(bell dings)

00:03:31.211 --> 00:03:32.721
I'm gonna do ecclesiastical and obvious.

00:03:32.721 --> 00:03:33.554
(buzz)

00:03:33.554 --> 00:03:35.136
And no I didn't just get that
answer cause I don't know

00:03:35.136 --> 00:03:37.630
what ecclesiastical means or tepid means.

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:40.565
(up-beat music)

00:03:40.565 --> 00:03:43.369
Yeah I wonder if they did
have classes back in the day,

00:03:43.369 --> 00:03:46.026
they're like okay, here's
your next cigarette problem.

00:03:46.026 --> 00:03:48.478
SAT and all of the
people who come together

00:03:48.478 --> 00:03:51.860
to make it, realize that
time is changing really fast.

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:55.840
Honestly, thank goodness I did
not have to take this test.

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:58.294
I promise I did better on the real test.

00:03:58.294 --> 00:04:00.427
If they would have
worded them differently,

00:04:00.427 --> 00:04:02.634
I probably would have
got the better answers.

00:04:02.634 --> 00:04:04.790
What was my score equivalent to?

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:05.675
Like what college would I have gotten

00:04:05.675 --> 00:04:07.816
into with that percentage?

00:04:07.816 --> 00:04:10.483
(up-beat music)

00:04:18.422 --> 00:04:20.633
(mechanical whooshes and creaks)

